SS3_B = {
"English Language": [
 ("Lexis & Register",
  [("Introduction", ["Lexis is the total vocabulary of a language; lexicology studies words, their meanings, structures and relationships. Register is the variety of language appropriate to a situation — formal, informal or technical."]),
   ("Word formation", ["Words are formed by <b>derivation</b> (adding affixes: happy → unhappy, happiness), <b>compounding</b> (two words: headmaster, notebook), <b>conversion</b> (change of class: to email, to Google), <b>blending</b> (brunch = breakfast + lunch), <b>clipping</b> (advertisement → advert), <b>acronyms and initialisms</b> (WAEC, NEPA), and borrowing from other languages (kola, yam from West Africa)."]),
   ("Register and its varieties", ["<b>Formal register</b> — standard, impersonal, passive and latinate vocabulary (official letters, speeches, essays).", "<b>Informal register</b> — conversational, contractions, slang (texts to friends).", "<b>Technical register</b> — the jargon of a discipline: 'acid, titration' in chemistry; 'defendant, appeal' in law; 'server, cache' in computing. Choosing the right register shows language competence."]),
   ("Usage and misuse", ["Distinguish similar words: affect/effect, principal/principle, stationary/stationery, complement/compliment, council/counsel. Avoid illiteracies like 'irregardless', double comparatives ('more better') and wrong collocations ('make a research' → 'carry out research')."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define register.", "Give one example each of formal, informal and technical register.", "Form new words from 'write' using affixes.", "Use 'complement' and 'compliment' in sentences."])]),
 ("Synonyms, Antonyms & Homonyms",
  [("Introduction", ["Synonyms are words of similar meaning; antonyms are words of opposite meaning; homonyms are words that share spelling or pronunciation but differ in meaning."]),
   ("Synonyms with shades of meaning", ["Few words are exact synonyms — each carries a shade: big, large, huge, enormous differ in degree; shout, yell, scream differ in intensity and context; begin, commence, initiate differ in formality. Choose the precise word: 'The teacher reproved the pupil' (gently) vs 'reprimanded' (officially)."]),
   ("Antonyms", ["Gradable (hot/cold, rich/poor), complementary (dead/alive, married/single) and relational (buy/sell, teacher/student). An antonym is usually formed with a prefix: honest → dishonest, legal → illegal, responsible → irresponsible, patient → impatient."]),
   ("Homonyms, homophones and homographs", ["<b>Homophones</b> — same sound, different spelling/meaning: right/write, their/there, site/sight, peace/piece, affect/effect.", "<b>Homographs</b> — same spelling, different sound/meaning: lead (metal / to guide), tear (eye drop / to rip), wind (air / to twist).", "<b>Homonyms</b> — same spelling and sound but different meanings: bank (river / money), bat (animal / sports tool), watch (timepiece / observe)."]),
   ("Context in use", ["The meaning of a homonym is decided by context: 'The bank of the river was full of marsh grass.' Only context disambiguates such words."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define synonym, antonym and homonym.", "Write the antonyms of: legal, patient, honest, happy.", "Distinguish between homophones and homographs with two examples each.", "Use 'bark' in two sentences showing two meanings."])]),
 ("Emphatic Structures",
  [("Introduction", ["Emphatic structures are grammatical devices used to place special stress on a part of a sentence — to highlight, correct or contrast information."]),
   ("Devices of emphasis", ["<b>It-cleft:</b> 'It was John who broke the cup.' (focus on John)", "<b>Wh-cleft/pseudo-cleft:</b> 'What I need is more time.' (focus on the complement)", "<b>Inversion:</b> 'Never have I seen such a sight.'; 'Rarely does he complain.'; 'Not only did she pass, she topped the class.'", "<b>Emphatic do/does/did:</b> 'I do love this school.'; 'He did apologise.'", "<b>Intensifiers and fronting:</b> 'This, I must say, is remarkable.'; 'Very/terribly/extremely good.'", "<b>Rhetorical repetition and contrast:</b> 'It was not the money, but the principle.'"]),
   ("Using emphasis in speech and writing", ["In speech, stress and intonation carry emphasis ('JOHN broke the cup'). In writing, cleft and inversion structures are more formal and accurate than italics or capitals. Emphatic structures appear in comprehension ('what was emphasised?') and in continuous writing."]),
   ("Common errors", ["Avoid 'It is me who is...' confusion; ensure the verb agrees with the focused subject: 'It is the students who are late.' Maintain word order after inversion: 'Seldom have I seen...' (not 'Seldom I have seen...')."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Rewrite 'John stole the money' using an it-cleft focusing on John.", "Transform 'I have never seen a better match' using inversion.", "What does emphatic 'do' express? Give an example.", "Why are cleft structures preferred in formal writing?"])]),
 ("Expository Essays",
  [("Introduction", ["An expository essay explains, informs or describes a topic objectively — it presents facts, defines concepts and clarifies ideas. Examples: 'The importance of education', 'How to prepare for WAEC', 'Types of pollution'."]),
   ("Structure", ["<b>Introduction</b> — a clear statement of the topic and the writer's approach (3–4 sentences, ending with the thesis).", "<b>Body</b> — 3–5 paragraphs, each with a topic sentence followed by explanations, examples, facts, statistics or definitions in a logical order (chronological, cause-effect, problem-solution, compare-contrast).", "<b>Conclusion</b> — a summary of the main points and a final thought; no new ideas."]),
   ("Language of exposition", ["Third person; formal, impersonal tone ('It is generally agreed...'); present tense for general truths; precise vocabulary; linking words of addition (furthermore), contrast (however), cause (therefore) and sequence (firstly, next); objective, not emotional, language."]),
   ("Planning and pitfalls", ["Plan before writing: list points, group related ones, order them, write, check. Pitfalls: straying from the topic, weak topic sentences, unsupported claims, informal language, and a conclusion that introduces new matter. Keep within the word limit; write neat, legible work."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Write the introductory paragraph of an exposition on 'The importance of reading'.", "What is a topic sentence?", "List three effective linkers for expository writing.", "State two pitfalls to avoid in an expository essay."])]),
 ("Argumentative Essays",
  [("Introduction", ["An argumentative essay presents a reasoned case for or against a proposition ('Boarding school is better than day school'). Its aim is to persuade, using evidence, logic and rhetoric, not emotion alone."]),
   ("Plan", ["<b>Introduction</b> — state the proposition and your stand clearly.", "<b>Body</b> — 3–5 supporting arguments, each in its own paragraph with a topic sentence, explanation, example/evidence, and relevance to the proposition.", "<b>Counter-argument and refutation</b> — acknowledge the other side and show why it is weaker ('Admittedly... however...').", "<b>Conclusion</b> — restate the stand with force and a call to action."]),
   ("Language of argument", ["Formal and persuasive; modal verbs of certainty (must, ought, surely); concessive linkers (although, even though, admittedly); causal links (because, consequently, therefore); rhetorical questions; statistics and expert opinion; parallel structures and balanced sentences. Never use slang or personal attack."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Outline an argument IN FAVOUR of 'Mobile phones should be banned in schools' in four paragraphs.", "What is a counter-argument and why must it be answered?", "Give two persuasive devices used in argumentative writing.", "State the purpose of the conclusion."])]),
 ("Comprehension & Summary: Exam Technique",
  [("Introduction", ["In WAEC/NECO, comprehension and summary questions test understanding, precision and economy of language. Good technique is as important as good English."]),
   ("Comprehension skills", ["Read the passage twice — first for the gist, then with the questions in mind. Answer in your own words unless asked to quote; full sentences; one idea per answer; match marks with points; note tone (the writer's attitude: sarcastic, admiring, critical) and the devices of persuasion."]),
   ("Summary technique", ["Identify the key points that answer the question; express each in one clause; paraphrase (never lift whole sentences); sequence the points as they appear; count the words and keep to the limit; if a figure is given ('in one sentence, state two reasons') obey it exactly."]),
   ("Common penalties", ["Copying chunks of the passage; exceeding the word limit; answering two questions in one; omitting points; wrong tense agreement; missing the instruction (e.g. 'state four' but giving three)."]),
   ("Practice passage plan", ["Read, underline key sentences, ask 'what is the writer saying about...?', draft the summary in your own words, edit for economy, check the instruction was followed, then write cleanly with a word count at the end."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Why should you read a comprehension passage twice?", "State three rules of summarising.", "What is tone in a passage?", "Why is copying from the passage penalised in summary questions?"])]),
],
"Geography": [
 ("World Regions: Africa",
  [("Introduction", ["Regional geography studies the physical and human features of a region. Africa, the second largest continent (about 30 million km²), lies mostly in the tropics, crossed by the equator and bounded by the Atlantic and Indian Oceans, the Mediterranean and the Red Sea."]),
   ("Physical features", ["Three main landform zones: the plateaus and basins of the interior (Congo Basin, East African Plateau), the high mountain ranges (Atlas in the north, the Rift Valley highlands with Kilimanjaro, Kenya, Ruwenzori), and the low coastal plains. Great rivers: Nile (longest), Congo, Niger, Zambezi. Lakes: Victoria, Tanganyika, Malawi. Hot deserts: Sahara (north), Kalahari and Namib (south)."]),
   ("Climate and vegetation", ["The equator gives high temperatures all year; rainfall controls vegetation belts: equatorial rainforest (Congo basin, West African coast), savanna (vast grasslands with scattered trees), desert (Sahara) and Mediterranean scrub (north and south tips), with montane vegetation in the highlands. Rainforests are disappearing through logging and farming (deforestation)."]),
   ("People and economic activities", ["Africa's population is over 1.4 billion, with a youthful profile, rapid urban growth and high birth rates. Economic activities: subsistence and commercial agriculture (cocoa, coffee, cotton, oil palm), mining (gold, diamonds, oil), forestry, fishing and growing services. Problems: poverty, debt, food insecurity, poor infrastructure, conflict and the impacts of climate change (drought in the Sahel)."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Describe the three landform zones of Africa.", "Name two rivers and lakes of Africa.", "Explain how rainfall controls African vegetation.", "State three economic problems facing African countries."])]),
 ("Regional Geography: West Africa",
  [("Introduction", ["West Africa is the region between the Sahara and the Gulf of Guinea (about 7.5 million km², 16 countries). Nigeria is the most populous, followed by Ghana, Ivory Coast, Senegal and the Niger Republic."]),
   ("Physical and climatic features", ["Landforms: the Fouta Djallon highlands (headwaters of many rivers), the Jos Plateau, the Adamawa highlands, coastal lowlands with lagoons and the Niger Delta. Rivers: Niger and Benue (navigable), Volta, Senegal, Gambia. Climate: the tropical continental (hot, dry north), tropical monsoon (south with heavy rains) and the Guinea-type coast; the alternation of the dry Harmattan winds and moist south-west monsoon controls the seasons and rainfall."]),
   ("Vegetation", ["Belts run roughly parallel to the coast: salt and freshwater swamp, tropical rainforest (with valuable timber: mahogany, obeche, iroko), Guinea savanna, Sudan savanna and Sahel (semi-desert). Overgrazing, logging and bush burning push the desert southward (desertification)."]),
   ("Economic life", ["Agriculture employs the majority of people: cash crops (cocoa in Ghana and Ivory Coast; palm oil in Nigeria; groundnut in Senegal; cotton), food crops (yam, cassava, maize, rice), fishing along the coast and inland waters, mining (gold in Ghana, iron ore in Guinea, tin in Nigeria, oil in Nigeria), trading and industries around coastal ports (Lagos, Tema, Abidjan, Dakar). ECOWAS promotes regional integration."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Name four West African countries.", "Which winds control the seasons of West Africa?", "Describe the vegetation belts from coast to Sahel.", "Why is cocoa important to Ghana and Ivory Coast?"])]),
 ("Comparative Studies (Nigeria & Ghana)",
  [("Introduction", ["Comparative geography examines the similarities and differences between two or more regions — usually in position, relief, climate, vegetation, population and economic development. This week compares Nigeria with Ghana (both British colonies, both oil/gold producers, both cocoa exporters)."]),
   ("Comparison", ["<b>Size/population:</b> Nigeria 923,000 km², 200m+ people; Ghana 238,500 km², about 33m — Nigeria is four times larger and far more populous. <b>Relief:</b> both have coastal plains, plateaus and river basins (Niger–Benue; Volta basin). <b>Climate:</b> both tropical, with the south wetter than the north; Ghana's coast has the drier 'dry equatorial' zone due to the cold Canary current. <b>Vegetation:</b> both run rainforest → savanna → Sahel. <b>Resources:</b> Nigeria — oil dominant; Ghana — gold, cocoa, and now oil and bauxite. <b>Economy:</b> both depend on primary products, with growing services."]),
   ("Conclusions of comparison", ["Comparisons show how physical environment and history shape development; they help planners learn from neighbours and expose the sources of regional imbalance. Nigeria's oil wealth has not yet produced the per-capita income of smaller, better-managed neighbours."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Compare the size and population of Nigeria and Ghana.", "Explain one similarity and one difference in their climate.", "Compare their main exports.", "What lessons can Nigeria learn from Ghana's economy?"])]),
 ("Map Interpretation",
  [("Introduction", ["Topographical maps represent the earth's surface — relief, drainage, vegetation, settlement and human activities — using conventional signs, contours and a scale. Interpreting them is a core exam skill."]),
   ("Key elements", ["<b>Scale</b> — the ratio between map and ground distance (1:50,000 means 1 cm = 500 m). Use the linear scale or the scale statement; distances are measured in straight lines (with a ruler) or along roads (with a string/map measurer).", "<b>Contours</b> — lines joining points of equal height above sea level: close contours = steep slope, wide spacing = gentle slope, concentric circles = hills/peaks, V-shapes pointing uphill = valleys, pointing downhill = spurs.", "<b>Conventional signs</b> — colours (blue water, green vegetation, brown relief, red roads) and symbols (bridges, churches, airports, wells, settlements); grid lines and grid references (4- and 6-figure) fix positions."]),
   ("Interpretation skills", ["Describe relief (high/low land, slopes), drainage (rivers, direction of flow), settlement patterns (dispersed, nucleated, linear), land use (farming, mining, forestry, towns), and the relationship between the physical environment and human activity. Calculated answers: gradient = vertical interval ÷ horizontal distance; density of settlement; bearing between points."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["What is the distance on the ground of 6 cm on a 1:50,000 map?", "How do contours show a steep slope?", "What do V-shaped contour patterns pointing uphill indicate?", "Describe the physical and human features of a map area from a given extract."])]),
 ("Weather & Climate (Revision & Application)",
  [("Introduction", ["Weather is the state of the atmosphere at a place at a particular time; climate is the average weather of a place over many years (30+). This week consolidates the instruments and the concepts behind weather maps."]),
   ("Elements and instruments", ["<b>Temperature</b> — thermometer (maximum–minimum, six's), in a Stevenson screen.", "<b>Rainfall</b> — rain gauge (funnel + measuring cylinder), recorded daily; the total over a month or year is the rainfall total.", "<b>Pressure</b> — barometer (mercury, aneroid); isobars join equal pressures on maps.", "<b>Wind</b> — anemometer (speed), wind vane (direction); <b>Humidity</b> — hygrometer/wet-and-dry bulb; <b>Sunshine</b> — Campbell–Stokes recorder; <b>Clouds</b> — observation; <b>Evaporation</b> — evaporimeter."]),
   ("Reading weather data", ["Mean daily temperature = (max + min) ÷ 2; diurnal range = max − min; annual range for a station from the monthly means. Describe climate from a climatic graph: hot and wet all year (equatorial); hot with summer rain (tropical wet and dry); cool winters (Mediterranean). The Nigerian climate graph shows two seasons — wet (April–October) and dry (November–March)."]),
   ("Application", ["Climate shapes agriculture (planting seasons), settlement, transport, health (malaria in wet zones) and economic planning. Nigerians plan planting around the onset of rains; the Harmattan brings dry, dusty days in the north."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define weather and climate.", "Name the instrument for rainfall and how it is read.", "Calculate the mean daily temperature: max 32 °C, min 21 °C.", "Describe the two seasons of Nigeria."])]),
 ("Environmental Hazards & Conservation",
  [("Introduction", ["Environmental hazards are natural or man-made events that threaten life and property. In Nigeria the major hazards are flooding, desertification, erosion, drought, deforestation and pollution — each worsened by human activity."]),
   ("Major hazards in Nigeria", ["<b>Flooding</b> — heavy rains and blocked drains (Lagos, Benin, Lokoja, 2012 and 2022 floods); rivers overflow; slums on flood plains suffer most.", "<b>Desertification</b> — the Sahara advances south because of overgrazing, bush burning and tree felling in the Sahel (hits Borno, Yobe, Sokoto, Katsina).", "<b>Soil erosion</b> — gully erosion in the east (Anambra, Imo) and sheet erosion elsewhere, from deforestation and poor farming methods.", "<b>Drought</b> — prolonged rain failure in the north, causing crop failure and famine. <b>Deforestation</b> — loss of forest cover for fuel and farmland. <b>Pollution</b> — oil spills in the Delta, industrial waste, refuse and air pollution."]),
   ("Causes and effects", ["Causes are mainly human: farming on marginal land, logging, overgrazing, poor urban planning, dumping of waste, gas flaring, construction on flood plains. Effects: loss of lives, homes and farmland; food shortage; siltation of rivers; loss of biodiversity; displacement of people; economic losses."]),
   ("Conservation measures", ["Afforestation and reforestation (the Great Green Wall across the Sahel); contour ploughing, terracing and cover cropping; controlled grazing; construction of drainage and flood control (dredging of rivers); tree planting campaigns; waste management (recycling); environmental laws and agencies (NESREA, forestry departments); public education and environmental impact assessment."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define environmental hazard.", "State three causes of desertification in the Sahel.", "List two effects of gully erosion.", "Mention three measures for conserving Nigeria's environment."])]),
],
"Government": [
 ("Pre-Colonial Nigerian States",
  [("Introduction", ["Before colonial rule, Nigeria was organised into empires, kingdoms, city-states and chiefdoms with well-developed systems of government. The major states were the Oyo Empire, Benin, Kanem-Bornu, Sokoto Caliphate, the Igbo societies and the Hausa city-states."]),
   ("The main pre-colonial states", ["<b>Oyo Empire (Yoruba)</b> — the Alaafin was the political head with the Oyomesi (council of chiefs) and the Bashorun as checks; the empire grew rich from trade and cavalry.", "<b>Benin Kingdom (Edo)</b> — the Oba ruled with the Uzama (hereditary chiefs), the Eghosa and the Iwebo; famous for brass art and a centralised administration.", "<b>Kanem-Bornu (Lake Chad)</b> — the Mai was the ruler, with the Magira (queen mother) and a council; the Trans-Saharan trade and Islam shaped it.", "<b>Sokoto Caliphate</b> — founded by Uthman dan Fodio (1804–1810); the Caliph (Sarkin Musulmi) headed the empire of emirates, each with an Emir applying Sharia.", "<b>Igbo societies</b> — decentralised: the village assembly and council of elders (Ozo title holders) ruled by consensus; the Ofala of Onitsha and the Eze of Nri were notable exceptions."]),
   ("Features of pre-colonial government", ["They had constitutions (customs and traditions), separation of functions (the Alaafin vs Oyomesi checks), rule of law (customary law), elections indirectly (oracle, primogeniture, selection by chiefs), taxation (tribute), and courts. Their weakness: no written records and vulnerability to slave raiding and internal wars."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Name four pre-colonial Nigerian states.", "What was the Alaafin's relationship with the Oyomesi?", "Describe the political system of the Igbo.", "How did the Sokoto Caliphate govern its emirates?"])]),
 ("The Civil War & Reconciliation",
  [("Introduction", ["The Nigerian Civil War (1967–1970) followed the secession of the Eastern Region as the Republic of Biafra. It was fought over the coup and counter-coup of 1966, the massacre of Easterners in the North, and the struggle over oil revenues and federal power."]),
   ("Causes", ["The 1966 military coups and the breakdown of trust between ethnic groups; the anti-Igbo pogroms in the North; the unilateral declaration of Biafra (30 May 1967) by Colonel Odumegwu Ojukwu; the federal blockade and the dispute over oil revenue allocation; the struggle for control of the central government."]),
   ("Course and effects", ["The war raged for 30 months ('the Nigeria–Biafra war') with heavy fighting in the Mid-West, the Rivers and the South-East; starvation and famine ('no victor, no vanquished' is often quoted) killed many civilians. Effects: loss of lives (estimated 1–3 million, mostly from hunger), destruction of industries and infrastructure, inflation, and deep ethnic wounds."]),
   ("Reconciliation and lessons", ["The war ended in January 1970 with the surrender of Biafra, and General Yakubu Gowon declared 'no victor, no vanquished'. Reconciliation was pursued through reintegration, 'reconstruction, reconciliation and rehabilitation' (the 3Rs), creation of more states, and the Federal Character principle in the constitution. Lesson: dialogue, justice, fairness and unity are precious; war destroys all sides."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["State three causes of the Nigerian Civil War.", "When was Biafra declared and by whom?", "Mention three effects of the war.", "How was reconciliation achieved after 1970?"])]),
 ("Nigeria's Foreign Policy",
  [("Introduction", ["Foreign policy is the sum of a country's goals and actions in international relations. Nigeria's foreign policy is guided by the constitution and articulated in the concept of 'Africa as the centrepiece of Nigeria's foreign policy'."]),
   ("Objectives and principles", ["The main principles: respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of states; non-interference in the internal affairs of other states; support for the independence of African states; promotion of African unity and development; peaceful settlement of disputes; support for international law and the UN; protection of Nigerians abroad; promotion of economic cooperation."]),
   ("Policy instruments", ["Diplomacy through missions abroad; membership of international organisations (UN, AU, ECOWAS, OPEC, Commonwealth, NAM); economic diplomacy (trade, investment, oil); military assistance and peacekeeping; cultural relations; and the media."]),
   ("Major achievements", ["Nigeria's leading role in the formation of ECOWAS (1975), peacekeeping in the Congo, Liberia (ECOMOG), Sierra Leone and The Gambia; the decolonisation struggle for Angola, Mozambique and Zimbabwe; debt relief campaigns; leadership in the AU; hosting of the UN headquarters in Africa's debates; and shuttle diplomacy for African conflicts."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define foreign policy.", "What is 'Africa as the centrepiece' of Nigeria's foreign policy?", "List three principles of Nigeria's foreign policy.", "Mention two achievements of Nigeria's peacekeeping efforts."])]),
 ("African Union & ECOWAS",
  [("Introduction", ["The African Union (AU) is the continental organisation of African states, and ECOWAS is the regional economic community of West Africa. Both promote unity, peace and development."]),
   ("The African Union", ["Founded in 2002 in Durban as the successor of the OAU (1963). Organs: the Assembly of Heads of State (highest), the Executive Council, the Commission, the Pan-African Parliament, the Court of Justice, the Peace and Security Council and specialised agencies. Objectives: unity, solidarity and cooperation among African states; accelerating integration; defending sovereignty; promoting human rights and democratic governance; and Agenda 2063 for sustainable development. Achievements: peacekeeping in Darfur and Somalia, the African Standby Force, and the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA)."]),
   ("ECOWAS", ["Founded by the Treaty of Lagos in 1975 — 15 member states. Objectives: economic integration (free movement of persons, goods and services; a common market; a single currency — the eco); and political cooperation (peace and security). Institutions: the Authority of Heads of State, the Commission, the Parliament (in Abuja), the Court of Justice, ECOWAS Bank for Investment and Development, and ECOMOG (the peacekeeping force that intervened in Liberia and Sierra Leone). Problems: uneven implementation, border closures against free movement, multiple currencies and coups."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["When was the AU founded and what did it replace?", "Name three organs of the AU.", "State two objectives of ECOWAS.", "What is ECOMOG and in which two countries did it intervene?"])]),
 ("The Nigerian Constitution & Fundamental Rights (Revision)",
  [("Introduction", ["This week consolidates the constitutional studies of the term: the supremacy of the constitution, the separation of powers and the fundamental rights every Nigerian enjoys under Chapter IV of the 1999 Constitution (as amended)."]),
   ("Key constitutional features", ["<b>Supremacy</b> — the constitution is the highest law; any law inconsistent with it is void. <b>Federalism</b> — power is shared between the Federal Government and 36 states plus the FCT. <b>Three organs</b> — the legislature (National Assembly), the executive (President and ministers) and the judiciary (courts), with checks and balances. <b>Amendment</b> — requires two-thirds of both chambers and two-thirds of the state houses of assembly."]),
   ("Fundamental rights", ["Right to life; dignity of the human person; personal liberty; fair hearing; private and family life; freedom of thought, conscience and religion; freedom of expression and the press; peaceful assembly and association; freedom of movement; freedom from discrimination. Rights are enforceable in court (fundamental rights enforcement procedure) and may be restricted only by law for public safety, order, health or morality."]),
   ("Citizenship and duties", ["Citizenship by birth, registration or naturalisation. Duties: obey the law, pay taxes, defend the country, vote, respect the rights of others, keep public property and participate in national service (NYSC)."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["What is the supremacy of the constitution?", "How is the Nigerian constitution amended?", "List four fundamental rights.", "State four duties of a Nigerian citizen."])]),
 ("Public Service & Administration (Revision)",
  [("Introduction", ["The public service is the body of officials that implements government policies and delivers public services — the civil service, the military and the police, parastatals and public corporations."]),
   ("Structure and functions", ["The federal civil service is organised into ministries (Health, Education, Finance, Defence...), departments and agencies, headed by the President at the top, then ministers, permanent secretaries, directors and other officers. The civil service advises the government, implements policies, prepares the budget, collects revenue, keeps records and delivers services."]),
   ("Principles", ["Politically neutral and permanent; anonymous; impartial; based on merit, promotion and discipline; accountable to the government and the public."]),
   ("Problems and reforms", ["Red tape and delay; corruption; inefficiency and poor morale; over-staffing; political interference; inadequate funding and training. Reforms: the Udoji Commission (1974), the Civil Service Reforms of 1988, due process and the Bureau of Public Procurement, the SERVICOM programme and the Treasury Single Account (TSA)."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define the public service.", "State four functions of the civil service.", "Mention four principles of the civil service.", "List three problems of the Nigerian public service and one reform each."])]),
],
"Agricultural Science": [
 ("Fisheries & Fish Farming",
  [("Introduction", ["Fisheries is the science of catching, rearing and processing fish and other aquatic animals. Fish farming (aquaculture) is the cultivation of fish in ponds, tanks, cages or raceways under controlled conditions."]),
   ("Importance of fisheries", ["Fish is a cheap source of animal protein; fishing provides employment for coastal and riverine communities; it earns foreign exchange (shrimps, frozen fish exports) and replaces imported fish; fish scales, oil and offal feed livestock; it makes use of water bodies."]),
   ("Types and systems of fish farming", ["<b>Extensive</b> — natural water bodies, minimal inputs; <b>semi-intensive</b> — ponds with feeding and stocking; <b>intensive</b> — high stocking with complete feeds and aeration (earthen ponds, concrete tanks, cages in rivers/lakes, and fibre tanks). Common species in Nigeria: tilapia, catfish (Clarias, Heterobranchus), carp, mudfish."]),
   ("Managing a fish farm", ["Site: good water supply, clay soil, flat land near a market, away from flooding. Pond preparation: clear and lime the pond, fertilise to grow plankton (natural feed), stock fingerlings at the right density, feed with floating pellets (5% of body weight daily), control predators and poachers, check water quality (pH 6.5–8.5, oxygen), harvest after 4–6 months with a net, then process fish (cleaning, smoking, freezing) before marketing."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define fisheries and aquaculture.", "State three importance of fish farming.", "List four species of fish reared in Nigeria.", "Describe how you would prepare a fish pond before stocking."])]),
 ("Apiculture & Snail Rearing",
  [("Introduction", ["Apiculture is the keeping of bees (for honey, beeswax and pollination); snail rearing is heliciculture — breeding snails for meat. Both are low-cost, small-space enterprises."]),
   ("Bee keeping", ["The hive: modern Langstroth hives give cleaner honey (movable frames); the colony has a queen (egg layer), workers (foraging, building, guarding) and drones (mating). Equipment: hive, smoker (calms bees), bee brush, veil and gloves, honey extractor. Siting: near flowers, away from people; facing away from footpaths; on stands. Management: inspect regularly, control pests (wax moth) and diseases, avoid disturbing in bad weather, provide water, harvest honey when capped (7–8% moisture), leave enough for the colony."]),
   ("Products and importance", ["Honey (food, medicine, sweetener), beeswax (candles, cosmetics, polish), propolis, royal jelly. Bees pollinate crops, raising yields; apiculture gives income and employment with little land."]),
   ("Snail rearing", ["Snails need a cool, moist, shaded pen with humus soil, calcium (for shells) and a fence (they climb); feed on leaves (cabbage, pawpaw, waterleaf), fruits and snail feed; control predators (rats, ants); harvest at maturity (about 8–12 months); process and market. Species: Archachatina marginata (giant land snail)."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define apiculture.", "State the three castes of a bee colony and their functions.", "List two products of the hive.", "What conditions and feed do snails need?"])]),
 ("Pasture & Forage Crops",
  [("Introduction", ["Pasture is land covered with grass and other plants grazed by livestock; forage crops are plants grown to be cut and fed to animals (hay, silage, fresh fodder). They are the cheapest source of feed for ruminants."]),
   ("Types of pasture", ["<b>Natural pasture</b> — unimproved grassland (woody shrubs and grasses); low in nutrients, seasonal, low carrying capacity. <b>Improved pasture</b> — planted with good grasses and legumes, fertilised, fenced and rotationally grazed; higher yields. <b>Rotational grazing</b> divides the pasture into paddocks grazed in turn, allowing rest and regrowth; <b>zero grazing</b> cuts and carries fodder to the animals."]),
   ("Common pasture grasses and legumes", ["Grasses: Panicum maximum (guinea grass), Cynodon dactylon (Bermuda grass), Andropogon gayanus (gamba grass), Brachiaria species, Pennisetum (elephant grass). Legumes: Centrosema, Stylosanthes, Pueraria (kudzu) and Leucaena. Legumes fix nitrogen, improving soil fertility and feed protein."]),
   ("Conservation of forage", ["<b>Hay</b> — grass dried in the sun until brittle, then stored dry (moisture below 20%). <b>Silage</b> — chopped green forage packed and fermented anaerobically in a pit or bag; the acid preserves it, best from maize or grass mixed with a little molasses. Hay for dry season roughage; silage keeps more nutrients."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define pasture and forage.", "Differentiate between natural and improved pasture.", "Name two legumes used in pastures and their value.", "How is silage made?"])]),
 ("Farm Produce Processing & Preservation",
  [("Introduction", ["Farm produce is perishable; processing and preservation prevent spoilage, add value, extend shelf life and stabilise prices. Methods are physical, chemical and biological."]),
   ("Why process and preserve", ["Reduces post-harvest losses (25–40% in Nigeria); makes produce available all year; adds value and income (garri from cassava, flour from wheat); improves quality, safety and transportability; creates jobs."]),
   ("Methods of preservation", ["<b>Drying/sun-drying</b> — fish, pepper, maize, cassava chips; <b>smoking</b> — fish (heat and smoke kill microbes); <b>salting</b> — fish and meat (salt draws out water); <b>refrigeration/freezing</b> — meat, fish, vegetables; <b>canning/bottling</b> — heat-sealed against microbes (tomato paste, fruits); <b>fermentation</b> — cassava into garri, milk into yoghurt, oil palm sap into wine; <b>chemical preservation</b> — salt, sugar, vinegar, sodium benzoate; <b>irradiation</b> and <b>pasteurisation</b> (milk) for longer life."]),
   ("Processing common Nigerian crops", ["Cassava → garri, fufu, starch, cassava flour; oil palm → palm oil, kernel oil, soap; cocoa → cocoa butter, chocolate; groundnut → oil, cake, butter; maize → flour, ogi, popcorn; rubber → latex, crepe. Processing requires clean water, hygienic handling and good storage (dry, ventilated, pest-free stores)."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["State two reasons for preserving farm produce.", "Differentiate between smoking and canning.", "How is garri made?", "Mention three chemical preservatives."])]),
 ("Agricultural Marketing & The Farmer's Business (Revision)",
  [("Introduction", ["Agricultural marketing is the process of moving farm products from the farm to the final consumer — assembling, grading, processing, packaging, transporting and selling. This week revises the business side of farming."]),
   ("Marketing channels and functions", ["Channels: farmer → local market → wholesaler → retailer → consumer (long chains, many middlemen). Functions: assembling (bulking produce), grading and standardising, processing, storage, packaging, transportation, financing, risk bearing and price discovery. The middleman takes a big share of the consumer's price — farmers get less than 50% in many cases."]),
   ("Problems of agricultural marketing", ["Poor storage and processing facilities; poor rural roads; price instability and seasonality; middlemen exploitation; lack of market information; inadequate credit; adulteration; poor packaging and grading."]),
   ("Farm business management", ["Planning (set goals, budget, choose enterprises); records (inventory, production, sales, cash book); costing (fixed and variable costs; break-even point); pricing; risk management (insurance, diversification, safe storage); and marketing strategy (cooperatives, guaranteed minimum price, commodity boards historically). Cooperative societies pool produce for better prices and bulk buying."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define agricultural marketing.", "List five marketing functions.", "State four problems of agricultural marketing.", "How can a cooperative help the farmer?"])]),
],
"Computer Studies": [
 ("System Development Life Cycle",
  [("Introduction", ["The System Development Life Cycle (SDLC) is the structured process by which information systems are planned, built and maintained. It turns a problem into a working software solution."]),
   ("The phases", ["<b>1. Problem definition/identification</b> — understand the user's problem and objectives.", "<b>2. Feasibility study</b> — is it technically, economically, operationally and legally possible? (cost–benefit analysis).", "<b>3. System analysis</b> — study the existing system, gather requirements (interviews, questionnaires, observation), produce the system specification.", "<b>4. System design</b> — design input, output, process, files and databases; draw diagrams (flowcharts, data flow diagrams, entity-relationship diagrams).", "<b>5. Implementation</b> — coding, testing, conversion (parallel, phased, direct or pilot) and installing.", "<b>6. Maintenance</b> — correcting errors, adapting to change, perfecting and preventing problems; also documentation and training end users."]),
   ("Models", ["The waterfall model (phases in strict order); the iterative/incremental model; the spiral model (risk-driven); prototypes (a working model shown to users early)."]),
   ("Importance", ["The SDLC prevents costly errors, keeps projects on time and within budget, produces quality software and gives clear documentation for future maintenance."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define the SDLC.", "List the six phases in order.", "What is a feasibility study and why is it done?", "Differentiate between parallel and phased conversion."])]),
 ("Data Security & Privacy",
  [("Introduction", ["Data security is the protection of data against unauthorised access, alteration, destruction and disclosure; privacy is the right of individuals to control what personal information is collected and how it is used."]),
   ("Threats to data", ["<b>Physical</b> — theft of devices, fire, flood, power surges, hardware failure. <b>Human</b> — careless staff, social engineering (phishing), insider attacks, password sharing. <b>Technical</b> — hacking, malware, spyware, ransomware, denial of service, data interception on unsecured networks."]),
   ("Security measures", ["<b>Access control</b> — passwords, PINs, biometrics (fingerprints, face), two-factor authentication, access rights by role.", "<b>Encryption</b> — scrambling data so only authorised users can read it (HTTPS, file encryption).", "<b>Firewalls and antivirus</b> — block intrusions and remove malware; <b>backups</b> — regular copies, off-site, tested restores; <b>updates and patches</b>; <b>physical controls</b> — locks, CCTV, restricted rooms; <b>policies</b> — acceptable-use rules, staff training."]),
   ("Privacy rights and law", ["Personal data (names, contacts, health, finances) must be collected lawfully, used only for its purpose, kept accurate and secure, and deleted when no longer needed. Laws: Nigeria's Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023 (and NDPR); the GDPR of the EU; organisations must obtain consent and guard against identity theft."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define data security and privacy.", "List three human threats to data.", "Explain encryption and why it matters.", "State two rights of a data subject under Nigerian law."])]),
 ("Computer Viruses & Protection",
  [("Introduction", ["A computer virus is a malicious program that attaches itself to other files and replicates, damaging or disrupting the computer. Malware in general includes viruses, worms, Trojans, ransomware, spyware and adware."]),
   ("Types of malware", ["<b>Virus</b> — attaches to a host file, spreads when the file runs (boot sector, macro, file viruses). <b>Worm</b> — self-replicating, spreads over networks without a host. <b>Trojan</b> — hides inside a seemingly useful program. <b>Ransomware</b> — encrypts your files and demands payment. <b>Spyware/keyloggers</b> — secretly record keystrokes and spy on activity. <b>Adware</b> — floods the screen with ads. <b>Rootkits</b> — hide deep in the system to control it secretly."]),
   ("How they spread", ["Email attachments and links; infected flash drives and downloads; malicious websites; pirated software; unpatched software vulnerabilities; Bluetooth and network shares; social engineering. Symptoms: slow performance, pop-ups, files disappearing or changing, unexpected messages, unusual network activity."]),
   ("Protection and control", ["Install and update antivirus/anti-malware software; run it regularly; keep the OS and apps patched; avoid unknown attachments and pirated software; back up data; use strong passwords and firewalls; be alert to phishing; educate users; restrict downloads; and disconnect infected machines from the network immediately."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define a computer virus.", "Differentiate between a virus and a worm.", "State two symptoms of malware infection.", "List four ways to protect a computer from viruses."])]),
 ("Emerging Technologies (AI, Cloud Computing)",
  [("Introduction", ["Emerging technologies are new tools changing how we compute, work and live. Major areas: Artificial Intelligence (AI), cloud computing, the Internet of Things (IoT), blockchain, big data and virtual/augmented reality."]),
   ("Artificial Intelligence", ["AI is the simulation of human intelligence by machines — learning, reasoning, perception and problem-solving. Branches: machine learning (systems learn from data), natural language processing (chatbots, translation), computer vision (face recognition), robotics, expert systems and speech recognition. Applications: assistants (Siri, Alexa), recommendation engines, self-driving cars, medical diagnosis, fraud detection. Benefits: speed, accuracy, automation. Risks: job displacement, bias in decisions, privacy, and misuse."]),
   ("Cloud computing", ["Using remote servers over the internet to store, manage and process data, instead of local machines. Service models: <b>IaaS</b> (infrastructure), <b>PaaS</b> (platform), <b>SaaS</b> (software — Google Workspace, Microsoft 365). Deployment: public, private, hybrid clouds. Benefits: access anywhere, scalability, pay-as-you-go, automatic updates, collaboration. Risks: internet dependency, security and privacy, vendor lock-in, cost at scale."]),
   ("Other technologies", ["<b>IoT</b> — everyday devices connected to the internet (smart homes, wearables). <b>Blockchain</b> — a tamper-proof distributed ledger (cryptocurrencies, supply chains). <b>Big data</b> — analysing huge data sets for insights. <b>VR/AR</b> — immersive training and games. <b>5G</b> — faster mobile broadband enabling these technologies."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define AI and name three of its branches.", "State two benefits and two risks of AI.", "Define cloud computing and name its three service models.", "What is the IoT? Give two examples."])]),
 ("Computer Applications (Revision & Application)",
  [("Introduction", ["This week revises how computers are applied across sectors, and the practical skills needed for the WASSCE/NECO computer studies paper: word processing, spreadsheets, presentations and the internet."]),
   ("Applications of computers", ["<b>Education</b> — e-learning, CBT exams, educational software. <b>Banking</b> — ATMs, electronic transfers, mobile banking, POS. <b>Medicine</b> — patient records, diagnosis imaging, telemedicine. <b>Business</b> — accounting, payroll, e-commerce, inventory. <b>Government</b> — e-governance, INEC voter register, NIN enrolment. <b>Engineering</b> — CAD designs, simulations. <b>Communication</b> — email, social media, video conferencing. <b>Agriculture</b> — farm records, drones, market information."]),
   ("Practical skills", ["Word processing: create, edit, format, tables, headers/footers, spellcheck, mail merge, print documents (MS Word, Google Docs). Spreadsheets: enter data, formulas (=SUM, =AVERAGE), functions, charts, sorting/filtering (Excel, Google Sheets). Presentations: slides, themes, transitions, animations, speaker notes (PowerPoint). Internet: search, email, web pages, e-safety, file sharing, web conferencing."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["State two applications of computers in banking and education.", "Give the spreadsheet formula to add cells A1 to A10.", "What is mail merge?", "List three e-safety rules for social media."])]),
],
"Civic Education": [
 ("Responsible Citizenship",
  [("Introduction", ["A responsible citizen is one who knows and fulfils his duties while claiming his rights, and contributes to the growth and unity of the nation. Citizenship is both a legal status and a moral call."]),
   ("Qualities of a responsible citizen", ["Patriotism and loyalty; obedience to the law; honesty and integrity; respect for others and tolerance of differences; hardwork and self-reliance; willingness to serve (community service, national service); participation in politics and governance; protection of public property; environmental consciousness; and rejection of corruption and crime."]),
   ("Duties to the state", ["Paying taxes; obeying laws; voting; defending the country; reporting crimes; keeping public property; participating in civic programmes."]),
   ("Rights and responsibilities in balance", ["Rights (life, liberty, education, vote, fair hearing) are matched by duties; there are no rights without responsibilities. A citizen can only demand his rights if he performs his duties."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["State five qualities of a responsible citizen.", "List four duties of a citizen to the state.", "Why must rights and duties go together?", "How can a student show responsible citizenship at school?"])]),
 ("Leadership & Followership",
  [("Introduction", ["Leadership is the ability to influence and direct people towards a common goal. Followership is the willingness to accept and support leaders while contributing to the same goal. Both are needed for a healthy society."]),
   ("Types of leadership", ["<b>Autocratic</b> — the leader decides alone; quick but dictatorial.", "<b>Democratic</b> — the leader consults decisions; participatory and inclusive (the best for a democracy).", "<b>Laissez-faire</b> — the leader gives full freedom; can cause disorder.", "<b>Servant/transformational</b> — the leader serves and inspires positive change."]),
   ("Qualities of a good leader", ["Integrity, honesty and transparency; vision; courage; humility; good communication; fairness and justice; accountability; self-discipline; empathy and the ability to listen."]),
   ("Qualities of a good follower", ["Loyalty; obedience to lawful directives; constructive criticism, not blind obedience; hardwork; cooperation; honesty; respect for leaders and colleagues; willingness to speak the truth when leadership goes wrong."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define leadership and followership.", "Compare autocratic and democratic leadership.", "State four qualities of a good leader.", "How can a follower correct a leader's mistake without disloyalty?"])]),
 ("Public Accountability",
  [("Introduction", ["Public accountability is the obligation of public officials to account for their actions, decisions and use of public resources to the people they serve. It is the heart of good governance and the fight against corruption."]),
   ("Mechanisms of accountability", ["<b>Constitutional bodies</b> — the Economic and Financial Crimes Commission (EFCC), the Independent Corrupt Practices Commission (ICPC), the Code of Conduct Bureau (CCB), the Office of the Auditor-General, the Public Complaints Commission and the Ombudsman.", "<b>Institutions</b> — the National Assembly's oversight and Public Accounts Committee, the courts, the media, civil society and the Bureau of Public Procurement (due process).", "<b>Budgets</b> — the annual budget, TSA (Treasury Single Account), and the Fiscal Responsibility Act."]),
   ("Obstacles to accountability", ["Corruption and bribery; weak institutions; secrecy and lack of transparency; poverty and ignorance; political interference; slow judiciary; fear of victimisation; ethnic and godfather politics."]),
   ("Benefits", ["Public funds are used for the people; government becomes trusted; investment grows; services improve; the civil service works better."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define public accountability.", "Name three anti-corruption agencies.", "State two obstacles to accountability.", "Why is the mass media important for accountability?"])]),
 ("Civil Society & NGOs",
  [("Introduction", ["Civil society is the sphere of organised, non-state groups through which citizens pursue common interests — NGOs, trade unions, professional bodies, faith groups, the press, community organisations and student unions."]),
   ("Functions of civil society", ["Educating citizens and creating awareness (public enlightenment campaigns); defending human rights and exposing abuses; fighting corruption and holding government accountable; providing services (health, education, relief); promoting civic participation and voter education; mediating conflicts; protecting the environment; and influencing policy through advocacy."]),
   ("NGOs in Nigeria", ["Local NGOs (e.g. CLEEN Foundation, Enough is Enough, the Nigeria Labour Congress as a union, COBES, and faith-based bodies like the Christian Association of Nigeria and the Nigerian Supreme Council for Islamic Affairs) and international NGOs (Amnesty International, Transparency International, ActionAid, UNICEF, UNDP, Save the Children) working on rights, health, education and governance."]),
   ("Challenges", ["Fighting for funds and foreign dependence; government hostility or co-optation; lack of coordination; poor capacity; corruption within some groups; the risk of being used for political ends. Strengths: grassroots reach, speed, independence and expertise."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define civil society.", "State four functions of civil society.", "Give two examples of NGOs in Nigeria.", "Mention two challenges facing civil society."])]),
 ("National Consciousness & Patriotism (Revision)",
  [("Introduction", ["National consciousness is the awareness of belonging to a nation with common symbols, history and destiny; patriotism is the love and devotion to one's country shown in words and deeds."]),
   ("Symbols and identity", ["The flag (green-white-green: the green stands for agriculture/land, the white for peace and unity), the coat of arms (the eagle, the horses, the shield, the wreath and the motto 'Unity and Faith, Peace and Progress'), the national anthem, the pledge, the colours of the currency, and the festival days (Independence Day, 1 October; Democracy Day, 12 June; Armed Forces Remembrance Day, 15 January)."]),
   ("Ways of showing patriotism", ["Respecting national symbols; obeying laws and paying taxes; participating in elections and civic duties; defending the country; promoting unity and unity in diversity; rejecting corruption, tribalism and nepotism; celebrating national festivals; doing honest, excellent work; and promoting Nigeria abroad."]),
   ("Obstacles to national consciousness", ["Ethnic and religious divisions; corruption; regional imbalances; bad leadership; poverty and unemployment; foreign cultural influence; and fake news. Remedies: value re-orientation, good governance, education, social justice, and the practice of good citizenship."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define national consciousness and patriotism.", "What does each colour of the flag represent?", "State three ways a student can show patriotism.", "Mention two obstacles to national unity in Nigeria."])]),
 ("Civic Education and National Development (Revision)",
  [("Introduction", ["This week reviews how civic education contributes to national development: the knowledge, skills and values that make citizens productive, law-abiding and development-oriented."]),
   ("Components revised", ["The constitution and rule of law; fundamental rights and duties; democratic processes (elections, voting, party politics); public accountability and anti-corruption; values and virtues; national identity and symbols; leadership and followership; civil society; social justice; road safety and environmental education; and health and HIV/AIDS awareness as civic issues."]),
   ("Civic education and development", ["An informed citizenry demands good governance; educated voters choose better leaders; civic awareness reduces crime, corruption and intolerance; civic values (integrity, hardwork, unity) release the productive energies of the nation."]),
   ("Youth participation", ["Join clubs and student unions; participate in community service; vote and be counted; report corruption; avoid cultism, drug abuse and examination malpractice; use social media responsibly; and develop skills for self-employment."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["List five components of civic education.", "How does civic education reduce corruption?", "State three ways young people can participate in national development.", "Why are values important for development?"])]),
],
"Food & Nutrition": [
 ("Food Service in the Home",
  [("Introduction", ["Food service covers the planning, preparation, presentation and serving of meals. Home food service should be attractive, hygienic, economical and suited to the family's timetable and budget."]),
   ("Planning the menu", ["Consider the family's nutritional needs (age, activity, health), likes and dislikes, budget and season; balance the food groups; vary the menu; plan ahead for the week; and involve the family in deciding the menu."]),
   ("Steps in serving food", ["1. Set the table cleanly (plates, cutlery, glasses, napkins) with guests' or family members' places arranged neatly. 2. Wash hands; serve hot food hot and cold food cold. 3. Use clean serving utensils; choose suitable dishes and covers. 4. Place the main dish so everyone can reach it. 5. Serve in order — guests first, then the family (or serve from the kitchen). 6. Keep the serving area clean."]),
   ("Table layout and etiquette", ["Place: fork left, knife right (blade inwards), spoon right of the knife, glass above the knife, napkin on the side plate or beside the fork; beverages right, bread/butter plate left. Table manners: sit upright, chew with the mouth closed, ask for food politely, use the right cutlery, avoid reaching across the table, and thank the host."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["What should be considered when planning a family menu?", "List the steps in serving a family dinner.", "Where is the fork placed at a table setting?", "State four rules of table etiquette."])]),
 ("Entertaining & Occasion Meals",
  [("Introduction", ["Entertaining is receiving guests with food and drink. Occasion meals are special meals for events — naming ceremonies, births, birthdays, weddings, Christmas, Sallah, Easter, New Year and funerals."]),
   ("Types of food service", ["<b>Table service</b> — waiter service, silver service (served from a platter), family service (dishes pass round).", "<b>Buffet service</b> — guests serve themselves from a table; less formal, good for large parties.", "<b>Self-service/cafeteria</b> — a counter line. <b>Finger/fork buffet and cocktail service</b> — standing guests with small dishes."]),
   ("Planning an occasion menu", ["Consider the occasion, number of guests, culture, budget and the season; include a balance of foods (protein, staple, vegetables, fruit, drink and a sweet); plan to be simple and attractive; make a shopping list and a time plan; prepare what can be made in advance; and involve helpers."]),
   ("Nigerian occasion dishes", ["Jollof rice and fried rice with chicken/fish; pounded yam, semo or amala with soups (egusi, ogbono, melon, afang); moi-moi, fried plantain; small chops (puff-puff, samosa, spring rolls, peppered gizzard); chin-chin, zobo, Chapman; fruit salad; traditional cakes and sweets."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define entertaining.", "Differentiate between buffet and table service.", "List the points to consider in planning an occasion menu.", "Name three Nigerian party dishes you would serve at a birthday and why."])]),
 ("Food Science: Emulsions & Leavening",
  [("Introduction", ["Food science explains the changes that occur in food during preparation. This week covers emulsions (mixing oil and water) and leavening (making mixtures rise) — both central to sauces, mayonnaise, cakes and bread."]),
   ("Emulsions", ["An emulsion is a mixture of two immiscible liquids (oil and water), one dispersed in the other in tiny droplets. Oil-in-water (milk, mayonnaise) and water-in-oil (butter) emulsions. Without an emulsifier the droplets separate. Emulsifiers (egg yolk in mayonnaise, lecithin, mustard, mono- and diglycerides) coat the droplets and keep them suspended. Emulsions separate on standing, freezing or heating; beating and gradual addition make them stable."]),
   ("Leavening agents", ["Agents that produce gas in a dough or batter, making it light and porous: <b>biological</b> — yeast (fermentation: sugar → CO2 + alcohol, in bread); <b>chemical</b> — baking powder and baking soda (with acid, e.g. buttermilk, releasing CO2 in cakes and pancakes); <b>physical/mechanical</b> — beating and folding air into mixtures (sponges, meringues); <b>steam</b> — water turning to vapour in batter (pancakes, Yorkshire pudding)."]),
   ("Factors for perfect results", ["Correct proportions and accurate measuring; fresh leavening agents; the right oven temperature (too hot — crust browns before the centre is done; too cool — a heavy cake); folding not stirring (to keep air); and the right flour (strong flour for bread, soft flour for cakes)."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define an emulsion.", "What is the function of an emulsifier? Give an example.", "Name three types of leavening with an example of each.", "Why is the oven temperature important in cake making?"])]),
 ("Food Additives & Labelling",
  [("Introduction", ["Food additives are substances deliberately added to food in small quantities to preserve, flavour, colour or improve it. Food labelling declares what a product contains so the consumer can choose safely."]),
   ("Types of additives", ["<b>Preservatives</b> — salt, sugar, vinegar, sodium benzoate, nitrites, sorbates (stop microbes).", "<b>Antioxidants</b> — vitamin C (E300), vitamin E — prevent fats going rancid. <b>Flavourings and flavour enhancers</b> — vanilla, monosodium glutamate (MSG, E621). <b>Colourings</b> — natural (annatto, beetroot) and synthetic (tartrazine, E102). <b>Sweeteners</b> — sugar, aspartame, saccharin. <b>Emulsifiers and stabilisers</b> — lecithin, gums. <b>Thickeners</b> — starch, pectin. <b>Acidulants</b> — citric acid, acetic acid."]),
   ("Why additives are used", ["To preserve food and prevent spoilage; to improve taste, colour and appearance; to maintain texture and consistency; to enrich (vitamins and minerals — fortification); and to make processing easier. They must be safe in the quantities used (acceptable daily intake)."]),
   ("Reading food labels", ["A good label shows: the product name, ingredients in descending order of quantity, net weight/volume, the manufacturer's name and address, country of origin, batch/best-before date, storage instructions, and nutritional information (energy, protein, fat, carbohydrate, sodium). Additives carry E-numbers. Labels matter for allergies, weight control, religious food rules (halal/kosher) and avoiding harmful additives."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define food additive.", "List six types of additives with one example each.", "Why are preservatives added to food?", "State five pieces of information required on a food label."])]),
 ("Food Commodities: Buying in Season & Storage (Revision)",
  [("Introduction", ["This week revises the purchase, storage and preservation of food commodities so that the household eats well at low cost throughout the year."]),
   ("Principles of wise buying", ["Buy in season (cheaper and fresher); buy at reputable markets; inspect quality (freshness, colour, no bruises, no pests); compare prices; buy the right quantity; make a list before shopping; avoid impulse buying; buy staples in bulk; keep receipts."]),
   ("Storage of commodities", ["<b>Cereals and flours</b> — cool, dry, airtight containers, off the floor; check for weevils. <b>Roots and tubers</b> (yam, cassava) — cool, dry, ventilated, away from light. <b>Fruits and vegetables</b> — cool place or refrigerator, wash before use. <b>Meat and fish</b> — refrigerator or freezer, or smoke/salt. <b>Fats and oils</b> — closed dark bottles away from heat. <b>Milk and dairy</b> — refrigerate, use by date. <b>Canned goods</b> — check expiry, avoid dented tins."]),
   ("Using leftovers", ["Refrigerate quickly, cover, use within 24 hours, reheat thoroughly to boiling, and never reheat more than once."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Why is buying in season economical?", "State four points to check when buying fish.", "How are cereals best stored?", "What is the correct way to store and reuse leftovers?"])]),
],
}
